WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.543 --> 00:00:02.877
“Now I am become death.”

00:00:03.795 --> 00:00:05.005
“The destroyer of worlds.”

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:09.217
P.O. Box 1663 was listed

00:00:09.217 --> 00:00:12.429
as a Santa Fe, New Mexico address in 1943.

00:00:14.305 --> 00:00:17.559
And over the next few years, 
about 300 babies...

00:00:17.809 --> 00:00:20.145
had it listed as the place of birth...

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:21.855
on their birth certificate.

00:00:23.773 --> 00:00:25.775
Because the real location was a secret.

00:00:25.942 --> 00:00:28.611
Everything sent to that P.O. box
ended up here:

00:00:28.611 --> 00:00:31.573
33 miles from Santa Fe at a site 
also known... 

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:35.744
as P.O. Box 180, Project Y, and 

00:00:35.744 --> 00:00:38.246
Los Alamos, New Mexico.

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:41.708
A secret city had been built there.

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:45.045
And it was home to a community
of scientists.

00:00:45.253 --> 00:00:46.755
“Scientists of many nations.”

00:00:46.963 --> 00:00:49.841
The scientists who created 
the first nuclear bomb.

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:52.677
They lived a couple hundred miles from the site

00:00:52.677 --> 00:00:54.220
where their invention would be tested.

00:00:54.262 --> 00:00:55.305
“New Mexico desert.”

00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:56.264
Trinity.

00:00:57.098 --> 00:01:00.268
How did laboratory director 
J. Robert Oppenheimer...

00:01:00.602 --> 00:01:01.936
end up building a town...

00:01:02.479 --> 00:01:04.481
and testing the first nuclear bomb...

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:06.775
here?

00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:11.613
Albert Einstein sent this letter 
on August 2nd, 1939.

00:01:11.654 --> 00:01:14.324
He sent it to President Franklin Delano Roosevelt.

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.785
Drawing from the work of physicists

00:01:16.785 --> 00:01:19.120
Enrico Fermi and Leo Szilard...

00:01:19.537 --> 00:01:22.415
Einstein warned of a nuclear chain reaction

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:24.292
in a large mass of uranium.

00:01:24.292 --> 00:01:25.794
“The splitting of the uranium atom...”

00:01:25.835 --> 00:01:29.464
Which could lead to extremely powerful bombs
of a new type.

00:01:29.464 --> 00:01:32.592
Bombs that either side might develop and use.

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:34.844
After a couple of years of study

00:01:34.844 --> 00:01:37.597
as well as the American entry into World War II,

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:41.351
in June 1942, the Army Chief of Staff

00:01:41.351 --> 00:01:43.686
established a temporary headquarters...

00:01:43.978 --> 00:01:46.564
at 270 Broadway in New York City.

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:48.399
The Manhattan Project had begun.

00:01:48.566 --> 00:01:51.277
And it was called a new "Manhattan District"

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:52.987
for the Army Corps of Engineers.

00:01:53.238 --> 00:01:55.740
This map shows contemporary boundaries

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.910
 for Army engineer districts.
Administrative areas.

00:01:59.035 --> 00:02:02.497
The Manhattan District encompassed 
all these smaller districts

00:02:02.914 --> 00:02:05.792
because of its larger scope: to build an atomic weapon.

00:02:07.836 --> 00:02:10.380
Less prominent secret locations included

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.925
a nuclear reactor under a 
University of Chicago football field...

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:17.178
the Alabama Ordnance Works 
for producing heavy water...

00:02:17.428 --> 00:02:18.596
and many others.

00:02:18.596 --> 00:02:23.017
1942 and 1943 saw the establishment 
of three major sites.

00:02:23.518 --> 00:02:26.813
It began with Oak Ridge, Tennessee, 
sometimes called Y-12.

00:02:27.105 --> 00:02:28.690
A large plant for the enrichment

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.109
of uranium and production of some plutonium.

00:02:31.359 --> 00:02:33.444
Nestled between mountains, it became...

00:02:33.444 --> 00:02:37.407
“...a city where 75,000 people 
worked in absolute secrecy

00:02:37.407 --> 00:02:39.701
on history's most sensational secret.”

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:43.496
Two other major locations 
were established in 1943.

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:46.749
The Hanford Engineer Works 
in Washington state

00:02:46.749 --> 00:02:49.377
was responsible for much of 
the production of plutonium.

00:02:49.752 --> 00:02:51.546
The top of this water tower there read...

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:53.965
“Silence means security.”

00:02:53.965 --> 00:02:57.177
But Hanford and Oak Ridge were nothing 
without the third site.

00:02:57.343 --> 00:03:00.138
The army needed a place 
to create the bomb.

00:03:02.265 --> 00:03:03.975
This is the Los Alamos Lament...

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:07.854
a poem sometimes sung, about life
in Los Alamos...

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:10.565
written by technical Sergeant Ralph Gates.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:11.941
It begins...

00:03:11.941 --> 00:03:13.401
“I'm just a PO number.”

00:03:13.443 --> 00:03:14.736
Specific numbers vary.

00:03:14.736 --> 00:03:16.362
The third verse reads...

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:19.115
[woman’s voice singing]
“He put us on a mountain...

00:03:19.407 --> 00:03:21.743
...outside of Santa Fe...

00:03:22.285 --> 00:03:27.832
...where the only sign of wildlife 
are GI wolves at bay.”

00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:32.378
Oppenheimer, based in Berkeley...

00:03:32.587 --> 00:03:35.340
had believed that a central lab was key.

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:39.510
While they considered Oak Ridge 
and Chicago as lab locations,

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.137
neither was remote enough.

00:03:41.512 --> 00:03:44.474
An option near LA 
wasn’t isolated.

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:47.894
One closer to Reno could be hit 
by heavy snows.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.315
General Leslie Groves Jr. of 
the Army Corps of Engineers

00:03:52.315 --> 00:03:53.316
ran the project.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.319
Oppenheimer and Groves agreed...

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:59.989
that New Mexico offered 
the security of isolation...

00:04:00.281 --> 00:04:01.658
as well as familiarity,

00:04:01.991 --> 00:04:04.452
since Oppenheimer had spent time in the area.

00:04:05.495 --> 00:04:08.706
The ideal site sat on the Pajarito Plateau.

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:10.124
It was isolated

00:04:10.208 --> 00:04:13.795
but also protected by its altitude 
and surrounding geography.

00:04:14.003 --> 00:04:17.799
Jemez Springs, chosen first, 
proved to be too difficult.

00:04:18.132 --> 00:04:20.468
The land was too difficult to acquire

00:04:20.468 --> 00:04:22.220
and the terrain was too rugged.

00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:25.932
But nearby Los Alamos was atop 
a table land between mesas...

00:04:26.057 --> 00:04:30.228
which made it easy to control entry 
and control any accidents.

00:04:30.561 --> 00:04:34.607
Much of it was on already federally-owned land as well.

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:36.526
The only existing structure

00:04:36.526 --> 00:04:39.570
was a small school that had opened in 1935.

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:40.780
The owners sold.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.950
The Secretary of War wrote 
the Secretary of Agriculture

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.785
about the military necessity

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:49.205
of acquiring the remaining 
federally-owned lands.

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:55.003
The request was granted for 54,000 acres 
of a demolition range.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.005
Los Alamos was activated...

00:04:57.463 --> 00:04:59.799
on April 1st, 1943.

00:05:02.093 --> 00:05:05.054
P.O. Box 1663 transformed

00:05:05.054 --> 00:05:06.556
from an outdoorsy ranch school

00:05:06.556 --> 00:05:07.807
with buildings like this...

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:10.310
into a community doing

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.812
the most advanced research 
in the world.

00:05:13.688 --> 00:05:17.692
Roads were quickly developed, but 
the town was kept isolated.

00:05:17.859 --> 00:05:22.989
Population grew from 1500 people 
to 5700 by 1945.

00:05:23.031 --> 00:05:27.285
So rapid that hutments were a 
common form of accommodation.

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:30.580
Here you can see the wash drying 
by Quonset huts.

00:05:30.705 --> 00:05:32.915
Apartment buildings were also available.

00:05:33.041 --> 00:05:34.625
These accommodations mingled

00:05:34.625 --> 00:05:37.545
next to facilities for graphite fabrication...

00:05:37.712 --> 00:05:40.715
and the cyclotron
and Van de Graaff machines.

00:05:40.965 --> 00:05:45.345
In early years, Los Alamos housed 
the world's finest researchers.

00:05:46.554 --> 00:05:47.889
Here, Dorothy McKibbin

00:05:47.889 --> 00:05:51.851
in charge of receiving new personnel,
sits next to Oppenheimer.

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:54.520
He's chatting with physicist 
Victor Weisskopf.

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:56.647
Here's Enrico Fermi on a hike.

00:05:56.898 --> 00:05:59.359
And this is Edward Teller's 
ID badge.

00:05:59.442 --> 00:06:02.362
He was later called the father 
of the hydrogen bomb.

00:06:04.072 --> 00:06:06.657
The Medical Corps colonel wrote
Leslie Groves that

00:06:06.657 --> 00:06:11.287
this intellectual group created challenges 
for a military operation.

00:06:11.287 --> 00:06:15.750
"The large percentage of intellectuals...
will require and seek

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.127
more medical care than the average person.”

00:06:18.294 --> 00:06:19.253
Other challenges?

00:06:19.253 --> 00:06:22.799
One-fifth of the married women 
became pregnant in Los Alamos...

00:06:23.132 --> 00:06:25.301
making maternity wards a necessity.

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:28.388
The past and atomic future intersected.

00:06:28.638 --> 00:06:30.723
Ice was cut from nearby ponds...

00:06:30.973 --> 00:06:32.767
and stored in ice houses...

00:06:33.101 --> 00:06:35.728
because electric fridges were 
too hard to get.

00:06:35.978 --> 00:06:39.774
At the time, the Bendix washer 
was revolutionizing laundry.

00:06:39.941 --> 00:06:44.445
By 1943, a classified ad in 
the Santa Fe New Mexican

00:06:44.445 --> 00:06:49.242
was looking for one to be shipped 
to P.O. Box 1663

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:52.245
for wartime work. But cultural phenomena...

00:06:52.495 --> 00:06:55.289
as varied as they were, 
like this Los Alamos band...

00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:57.166
they had one real purpose:

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:58.960
Building a bomb.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:02.338
And they needed a place to test 
the bomb that they built.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:08.094
This is the base camp at Trinity site.

00:07:08.386 --> 00:07:10.680
A rapidly established headquarters created

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.640
for testing the first atomic bomb.

00:07:13.724 --> 00:07:14.976
The desert training center

00:07:14.976 --> 00:07:17.353
north of Rice, California, 
was runner up.

00:07:17.895 --> 00:07:21.607
But it wasn't isolated enough 
or close enough to Los Alamos.

00:07:21.858 --> 00:07:24.444
Located in the Jornada del Muerto Valley.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:27.905
The winning site was selected
with a more extreme version

00:07:27.905 --> 00:07:29.907
of the Los Alamos criteria.

00:07:30.032 --> 00:07:32.452
Flat terrain to minimize blast effects.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.371
Isolated, yet close enough 
to Los Alamos.

00:07:35.746 --> 00:07:38.958
Good weather and nearby
to highways

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:41.335
like US-85 and 380.

00:07:41.752 --> 00:07:44.630
More than 200 residents settled at the camp.

00:07:45.256 --> 00:07:49.844
First, there was a 100 ton 
explosives test in May 1945.

00:07:50.261 --> 00:07:53.598
Then they prepared the 
Gadget nuclear device.

00:07:54.015 --> 00:07:56.642
And on July 16th, 1945...

00:07:56.893 --> 00:07:57.810
they conducted the test.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:01.481
“First try out of this new cosmic force 
was held on the New Mexico desert.”

00:08:02.356 --> 00:08:05.735
The Los Alamos Lament 
was written after that test...

00:08:06.235 --> 00:08:08.696
but before the August bombing of Japan.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:13.159
[woman singing]
“I’m just a P.O. number...”

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.245
“I have no real address...”

00:08:17.788 --> 00:08:20.500
“Although we were selected...”

00:08:20.917 --> 00:08:23.669
“I wonder for the best...”

00:08:24.045 --> 00:08:26.631
“We're not like other people...”

00:08:27.173 --> 00:08:29.634
“No one knows what we do...”

00:08:30.134 --> 00:08:32.970
“So P.O. Box 1663...”

00:08:33.763 --> 00:08:36.596
“Here's to you.”

